{"causes": ["The flourishing of Neoplatonism in the 3rd and 4th centuries CE provided a rich intellectual environment for philosophical inquiry and development.", "Iamblichus's establishment of a prominent Neoplatonic school in Apamea created a direct educational opportunity for talented students like Theodorus.", "The continued scholarly engagement with the works of Plato and Aristotle necessitated new commentaries and syntheses within the evolving Neoplatonic framework."], "effects": ["Theodorus's work helped to solidify and transmit the complex metaphysical and theological doctrines of Iamblichean Neoplatonism to subsequent generations.", "His philosophical interpretations significantly influenced later Neoplatonists, particularly Syrianus and Proclus, shaping the direction of the Athenian school.", "He contributed to the ongoing tradition of systematic commentary on classical Greek philosophy, integrating Platonic and Aristotelian thought within a unified Neoplatonic framework."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "iamblichus", "sourceName": "Iamblichus", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "theodorus-of-asine", "targetName": "Theodorus of Asine", "context": "Theodorus of Asine was a direct pupil of Iamblichus, learning and elaborating upon his teacher's Neoplatonic doctrines in the early 4th century CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "theodorus-of-asine", "sourceName": "Theodorus of Asine", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "ancient-greece", "targetName": "Ancient Greece", "context": "Theodorus of Asine's life and philosophical activity were rooted in the cultural and intellectual traditions of Ancient Greece, particularly in Laconia and the broader Hellenistic world."}, {"sourceSlug": "theodorus-of-asine", "sourceName": "Theodorus of Asine", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "syrianus", "targetName": "Syrianus", "context": "Theodorus of Asine's philosophical interpretations and commentaries were known and cited by Syrianus, teacher of Proclus, thereby impacting the Athenian Neoplatonic school in the 5th century CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "theodorus-of-asine", "sourceName": "Theodorus of Asine", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "neoplatonism", "targetName": "Neoplatonism", "context": "Theodorus of Asine was a central figure in the development and transmission of Neoplatonic philosophy, particularly after the time of Iamblichus."}, {"sourceSlug": "theodorus-of-asine", "sourceName": "Theodorus of Asine", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "philosophical-commentaries", "targetName": "Philosophical Commentaries", "context": "Theodorus of Asine produced extensive commentaries on the works of Plato and Aristotle, although most of these are now lost and known only through fragments."}], "places": [{"name": "Asine, Greece", "role": "Probable Birthplace"}, {"name": "Apamea, Syria", "role": "Center of Study"}, {"name": "Athens, Greece", "role": "Influenced School"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Theodorus of Asine", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "theodorus-of-asine"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q965333", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodorus_of_Asine"], "tags": ["Philosophers & Thinkers", "doctrine-development", "cause-and-effect", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Theodorus of Asine played a crucial role in the transmission and interpretation of Iamblichean Neoplatonism, directly influencing later prominent philosophers like Syrianus and Proclus and shaping the trajectory of late antique philosophy.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-09T00:59:23Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28986074567", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Theodorus of Asine was a Neoplatonist philosopher, and a native of one of the towns which bore the name of Asine, probably Asine in Laconia.", "newValue": "Theodorus of Asine, a prominent Neoplatonist philosopher who flourished in the early 4th century CE and died around 360 CE, was a pivotal figure in the post-Iamblichean development of this intellectual tradition. Hailing from one of the Greek towns named Asine, most likely Asine in Laconia, he became a direct pupil and close associate of Iamblichus, the Syrian founder of the Apamean school of Neoplatonism. Theodorus's significance lies in his role as a crucial conduit for Iamblichus's complex philosophical system, particularly his metaphysics and theology, helping to codify and transmit these ideas to the next generation of thinkers in the late Roman Empire.

Theodorus was known for his extensive commentaries on Plato and Aristotle, though regrettably, most of his original works are lost, surviving primarily through fragments and mentions in later Neoplatonic authors like Proclus. He engaged deeply with the intricate Neoplatonic hierarchies, particularly concerning the nature of the One, Intellect, Soul, and the sensible world, often clarifying or elaborating upon Iamblichus's sometimes obscure doctrines. His philosophical contributions included a refined theory of the Forms and their relation to the divine intellect, alongside detailed expositions on the structure of the cosmos and the human soul's ascent towards the divine.

The legacy of Theodorus of Asine is most evident in his profound influence on later Neoplatonists, particularly Syrianus, the teacher of Proclus, who frequently cited and engaged with Theodorus's interpretations. Through this intellectual lineage, Theodorus's understanding of Iamblichus's system became integrated into the foundational texts of the Athenian Neoplatonic school, ensuring his ideas continued to shape philosophical discourse for centuries. His role underscores the interconnectedness of late antique philosophical schools and the critical importance of pupils in transmitting and refining their masters' teachings."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-09T00:59:23Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28986074567", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "275 CE, Asine"}, {"title": "Ongoing philosophical inquiry and intellectual debate", "type": "Idea", "year": "275 CE, Asine"}], "newValue": ["The flourishing of Neoplatonism in the 3rd and 4th centuries CE provided a rich intellectual environment for philosophical inquiry and development.", "Iamblichus's establishment of a prominent Neoplatonic school in Apamea created a direct educational opportunity for talented students like Theodorus.", "The continued scholarly engagement with the works of Plato and Aristotle necessitated new commentaries and syntheses within the evolving Neoplatonic framework."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-09T00:59:23Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28986074567", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Contributed philosophical ideas to ongoing intellectual discourse", "type": "Idea", "year": "360 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "360 CE"}], "newValue": ["Theodorus's work helped to solidify and transmit the complex metaphy